'''Jingulu''', also spelt '''Djingili''', is an [[Australian Aboriginal languages|Australian language]] spoken by the [[Jingili people]] in the [[Northern Territory]] of Australia, historically around the township of [[Elliott District Community Government Council|Elliot]]. The language is an isolate branch of the [[Mirndi languages]].<ref>{{Cite book |last=Harvey |first=Mark David |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=TQwLAQAAMAAJ |title=Proto Mirndi: A Discontinuous Language Family in Northern Australia |date=2008 |publisher=Pacific Linguistics |isbn=978-0-85883-588-7 |location=Canberra |pages=3 |language=en}}</ref>

The Jingulu have (or had) a well-developed [[Manually coded language|signed form]] of their language.{{TOC limit |2}}

==Background and location==
Other languages spoken in the West Barkly family include [[Wambaya language|Wambaya]], [[Gudanji language|Gudanji]], [[Binbinka language|Binbinka]], and [[Ngarnka language|Ngarnka]]. When the [[Mudburra]] people arrived to the region where the Jingili live, a cultural fusion group arose named [[Kuwarrangu]], while the Jingilu and Mudburra cultures still remained separate. Based on geographical proximity, the Jingili and other ethnic groups have related languages with common vocabulary.{{sfn|Pensalfini|2003|pp=1-4}}

Jingulu was historically spoken around the township of [[Elliott District Community Government Council|Elliot]].{{sfn|Pensalfini|2003|pp=1-4}}

==Speakers and status==
Jingulu has an [[Ethnologue]] classification of [[moribund language|moribund]], meaning that it is an [[endangered language]], with only between 10 and 15 speakers in 1997,<sup>[[Jingulu language#cite note-ethnologue-4|[4]]]</sup> the youngest being in the fifties.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.ethnologue.com/about/language-status|title=Language Status|last=Eberhard|first=David M.|website=Ethnologue: Languages of the World}}</ref> An additional 20 people had some command of it. However, it was not used in daily communication which instead was conducted in either [[English language|English]] or [[Australian Kriol language|Kriol]].{{sfn|Pensalfini|2003|p=10}} In 2019 approximately five people still spoke the language, including [[Stuart Joel Nuggett]], who has recorded music in Jingulu.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Browning |first=Daniel |date=2019-08-23 |title=Can music revive endangered languages? |url=https://www.abc.net.au/radionational/programs/awaye/can-music-revive-endangered-languages/11443498 |access-date=2022-03-21 |website=ABC Radio National |language=en}}</ref> The remaining speakers are elderly.<ref name="ai">{{cite web |last=Perera |first=Alicia |date=2 August 2022 |title=Australian researchers develop new communication system inspired by rare NT Aboriginal language Jingulu |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-08-02/nt-aboriginal-language-jingulu-inspires-ai-system-jswarm/101266306 |access-date=3 August 2022 |website=ABC News |publisher=[[Australian Broadcasting Corporation]]}}</ref>

==Sign language==
{{main|Australian Aboriginal sign languages}}
The Jingulu have (or had) a well-developed [[manually coded language|signed form]] of their language.<ref>[[Adam Kendon|Kendon, A.]] (1988) ''Sign Languages of Aboriginal Australia: Cultural, Semiotic and Communicative Perspectives.'' Cambridge: Cambridge University Press</ref>

== Phonology ==

=== Vowels ===
