While there are only three phonemically distinct vowel phoneme qualities in Jingulu, the variations in vowel sounds are greater than in grammars with larger vowel phoneme inventories. These three phonemes have a variety of phonetic outputs depending on the word. The [[close vowel]] /i/ may be realized as {{IPAblink|i}}, {{IPAblink|ɨ}} or {{IPAblink|e}}; the [[close vowel]] /u/ most commonly as {{IPAblink|u}}, but also {{IPAblink|o}} and {{IPAblink|ɔ}}; and the [[open vowel]] /a/ as {{IPAblink|a}}, {{IPAblink|ʌ}}, {{IPAblink|æ}} and {{IPAblink|ə}}.{{sfn|Pensalfini|2003|p=21}}

Jingulu has contrastive vowel length. The orthographic convention of long high vowels is as two apparent syllable nuclei with a homorganic glide in between.{{sfn|Pensalfini|2003|p=22}} In orthography, {{IPA|/aː/}} appears as ⟨aa⟩. The other two are written (and optionally pronounced) with a [[Homorganic consonants|homorganic consonant]], {{IPA|/iː/}}~{{IPA|/i.ji/}} ⟨iyi⟩ and {{IPA|/uː/}}~{{IPA|/u.wu/}} ⟨uwu⟩, respectively.{{sfn|Pensalfini|2003|p=21}} Diphthongs of non-identical vowels in Jingulu are realized as separate syllable nuclei, i.e., {{angbr|ayi}}, {{angbr|awu}}, {{angbr|iya}}, {{angbr|iyu}}, {{angbr|uwa}} {{angbr|uwi}}.{{sfn|Pensalfini|2003|p=22}}

